{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791570606,"nonce":"u1/Fczio++VgxaPxc4/FFH/Z35Y=","title":"Python: inference copies a cached syntax scope for every statement it walks","message":"Walker::stmt asks Scope::of_body for the scope of each statement, every inference round, to read its bound names and whether it nests a body; infer_locals_with and list_param_facts ask for whole bodies. of_body caches the scope by body address but returns a clone: every HashSet\u003cString\u003e and nested child scope is copied on each hit.\n\nSampled at f3ed0900522166cda739a1ef257436bd7741cba1 on the Mac (ZYPY_PROFILE_STARTUP=200, go.py, 1 ms sampling): infer_module 4221 samples inclusive, as much as the shared lower_program (4192); allocation, free, memmove and string compares are about a quarter of inference's self time, and Scope::of_body is the largest single source of it (270 samples attributed, 241 of them from Walker::stmt).","files":null}]}